;margin:0 0 10px"><span>Just call it =E2=80=98energy security=E2=80=99</spa=
n></h2><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 =
0 15px"><span>Europe=E2=80=99s hottest summer on record. A collapsing glaci=
er in Nepal. Wildfires and droughts and freakish storms around the world.</=
span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:=
0 0 15px"><span>That=E2=80=99s what 1.5 degrees Celsius in warming above pr=
eindustrial levels looks like. It=E2=80=99s a number I found difficult to w=
rap my head around until a climate expert explained it to me this way: Imag=
ine your body temperature going up by 1.5 degrees. You feel unwell. Now ima=
gine it rising by 2 degrees =E2=80=94 you=E2=80=99re seriously ill.</span><=
/p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15=
px"><span>The planet is unwell. For many, that alone makes the case for agg=
ressively shifting away from fossil fuels to renewables.</span></p><p style=
=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>B=
ut even for those primarily interested in their bottom lines, the case has =
become pretty compelling. Renewable energy is the cheapest it=E2=80=99s eve=
r been. And it insulates countries from costly strategic dependencies.</spa=
n></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0=
 15px"><span>All of the above could be making the politics of the green tra=
nsition easier. Instead, across the West,</span><span style=3D"font-weight:=
700"> </span><span>they=E2=80=99ve become more difficult. Experts say even =
those committed to switching to renewables will often deliberately avoid th=
e word climate, talking instead about =E2=80=9Cenergy security=E2=80=9D or =
=E2=80=9Caffordability.=E2=80=9D</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25p=
x georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-weight:700"=
>A no-brainer=3F</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;=
color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>This reframing has the virtue of being tr=
ue.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;ma=
rgin:0 0 15px"><span>The wars in Ukraine and Iran have shown that relying o=
n unstable partners (Russia) and unstable regions (the Middle East) for ene=
rgy supplies makes countries vulnerable. At best, these countries could ris=
k a painful surge in their cost of living. At worst, shortages could destab=
ilize their politics.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,s=
erif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>And regardless of geopolitical tensi=
ons, many renewables increasingly win on cost alone, experts say. I spoke t=
o Wolfgang Blau, who advises businesses across the world on their climate s=
trategies. He told me that the technological progress of the last five year=
s had taken even optimists like him by surprise.</span></p><table border=3D=
