or:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>=E2=80=9CNot in our wildest dreams did we ex=
pect solar, wind and, especially, batteries to become so cheap and producti=
ve so fast,=E2=80=9D Blau said. China now generates nearly as much electric=
ity from renewables alone as the entire U.S. power system produces from all=
 sources. The U.A.E. and Saudi Arabia are building renewables at a pace and=
 scale that few would have predicted three years ago, Blau said.</span></p>=
<p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"=
><span>Companies can no longer ignore the climate, either. Significantly hi=
gher insurance premiums for flood or wildfire or heat-wave risks mean clima=
te change often shows up in the bottom line. Transportation costs have spik=
ed thanks to lower water levels in crucial waterways, from the Rhine to the=
 Panama Canal. If your supply chain depends on these, =E2=80=9Cthis affects=
 you and that=E2=80=99s because of climate change =E2=80=94 even if no one =
calls it that anymore,=E2=80=9D Blau said.</span></p><p style=3D"font:norma=
l 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span style=3D"font-w=
eight:700">Leaving the people behind</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px=
/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>But why are politicia=
ns and others in power so hesitant to talk about the climate=3F</span></p><=
p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px">=
<span>Right-wing populists on both sides of the Atlantic have taken aim at =
climate policies as a liberal elitist project that hurts working-class peop=
le.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;ma=
rgin:0 0 15px"><span>In Europe, the people campaigning against =E2=80=9Cnet=
 zero=E2=80=9D =E2=80=94 the goal of getting carbon emissions to zero =E2=
=80=94 accuse governments of driving up their energy bills. (Their argument=
s find traction in part because they=E2=80=99re not wrong. The process of c=
onstructing a grid that can efficiently distribute renewables comes with a =
price tag that some politicians have been reluctant to talk about, and that=
 will drive electricity bills up, not down, at least for a few years.)</spa=
n></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0=
 15px"><span>In the U.S., President Trump has aggressively doubled down on =
fossil fuels and calls climate change a =E2=80=9Choax.=E2=80=9D</span></p><=
p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px">=
<span>In both places, the politics around climate have created a pressure t=
o stop talking about it.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgi=
a,serif;color:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>Brussels increasingly pitches its=
 climate agenda as =E2=80=9Cenergy independence.=E2=80=9D In Britain, where=
 I live, a committee of British lawmakers last month suggested the governme=
nt drop the term =E2=80=9Cnet zero=E2=80=9D altogether, because it had beco=
me so toxic.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;colo=
r:#333;margin:0 0 15px"><span>I spoke to Katie White at our Climate Forward=
 conference in New York last week. She=E2=80=99s Britain=E2=80=99s minister=
 for climate transition, and even </span><span style=3D"font-style:italic">=
she</span><span> said she was comfortable using terms like =E2=80=9Cenergy =
security=E2=80=9D or =E2=80=9Cresilience,=E2=80=9D if they helped political=
ly.</span></p><p style=3D"font:normal 17px/25px georgia,serif;color:#333;ma=
rgin:0 0 15px"><span>=E2=80=9CIf people want to use a different word or a d=
ifferent phrase, or a different term, I=E2=80=99m fine with that,=E2=80=9D =
she said. =E2=80=9CAs long as we=E2=80=99re moving forward.=E2=80=9D (You c=
an watch our conversation </span><a href=3D"https://nl.nytimes.com/f/a/p0qX=
bJrDci8gyd70Qm8dYQ~~/AAAAARA~/GDTa_jDYpLZ3vHVaYzZo8kUVPfOcnY5ldrHtAQkS1jkKZ=
